++# What a stock Galaxy kernel needs that an AOSP one does not. All four are off by default: a device
++# that does not need them should not carry the code, and turning one on is a claim about the target
++# that a build cannot check for itself.
++config KSU_SAMSUNG_KDP
++    bool "Samsung KDP credential handling"
++    depends on KSU
++    default n
++    help
++      Install credentials through Samsung's KDP task bookkeeping instead of commit_creds()
++      directly, and release them the same way. Without this, a task's credentials and the
++      vendor's record of them disagree, which shows up as a root process that cannot be
++      signalled or reaped as expected.
++
++config KSU_SAMSUNG_RKP
++    bool "Samsung RKP sucompat bypass"
++    depends on KSU && KRETPROBES
++    default n
++    help
++      Reach setresuid and the su-compatibility syscalls through kretprobes on their own entry
++      points, for a target whose syscall table cannot be patched at all (the dispatcher stays
++      unavailable and the sys_enter tracepoint is never armed).
++
++config KSU_SAMSUNG_DEFEX
++    bool "Samsung DEFEX bypass"
++    depends on KSU
++    default n
++    help
++      Let tasks this module has elevated through Samsung's DEFEX enforcement, and re-sync a task
++      with it after the credential swap.
++
++config KSU_SAMSUNG_NO_PATCH_TEXT
++    bool "Disable text patching (Exynos EL2)"
++    depends on KSU
++    default n
++    help
++      Refuse every text patch instead of attempting one that the platform will fault. Set this on
++      a target where the hypervisor protects kernel text, so callers fail with an error rather
++      than half-succeeding.
++
+ menu "KernelSU - SUSFS"
+     depends on KSU_SUSFS
